  "Question": "A rectangle is enclosed with one side along a wall and a total length of 24 meters for the other three sides. The side perpendicular to the wall has length \\(x\\), and the side parallel to the wall has length \\(24-2x\\). What is \\(x\\) when the area is greatest?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "6",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Method 1: Use the derivative's sign\n\nThe sign of the area derivative tells us where the area increases or decreases. For a genuine rectangle, \\(0<x<12\\). \\(S=x(24-2x)=24x-2x^2\\), \\(S'=24-4x=0\\), so \\(x=6\\). Since \\(S'(x)>0\\) for \\(0<x<6\\) and \\(S'(x)<0\\) for \\(6<x<12\\), the area increases up to \\(6\\) and decreases afterwards. Hence its greatest value occurs when \\(x=6\\) meters.\n\nMethod 2: Complete the square\n\nBoth side lengths must be positive, so \\(0<x<12\\). Writing the area as a constant minus a square makes its upper bound visible:\n\\[\nS=x(24-2x)=72-2(x-6)^2\\le72.\n\\]\nEquality holds exactly when \\(x=6\\), which lies in the allowed interval. Thus the area is greatest when \\(x=6\\) meters.",
